1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:23,000
The video introduces a puzzle mechanic that captures part of a 3D world and places it somewhere new.

2
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Runtime cutting works on polygon faces, edges, and vertices, so simple low-poly level geometry is important for performance.

3
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:29,000
A plane divides space. Vertices are classified by side, crossing triangles are split, and new triangles seal the opening.

4
00:01:29,000 --> 00:02:16,000
The camera frustum determines what is preserved. Four thin border volumes define the cut planes and a larger frustum volume finds objects inside.

5
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:53,000
The demonstration level uses deliberately simple walls, floors, props, a portal, and a physical camera model.

6
00:02:53,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Distant scenery is real geometry for parallax and cutting. Baked lighting and unlit textures keep captured pieces visually consistent.

7
00:04:21,000 --> 00:05:08,000
The player can walk, jump, and turn while carrying the camera. A secondary camera renders the framed view, then freezes it at capture time.

8
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Separate camera masks and effects can reveal special objects or present the photographed world differently.

9
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Taking a picture preserves the geometry inside the frustum and parents it to the camera viewpoint so it travels with the player.

10
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Placing the picture cuts away the destination volume, activates the carried geometry there, and supports rotating the whole captured view.

11
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:46,000
The operation is computationally expensive, so the scene must stay lightweight and be designed around the core feature.

12
00:07:12,000 --> 00:08:00,000
The final gameplay demonstrates camera states, frozen photographs, rotated placements, and perspective-based paths through the level.
